 "title": "10 m platform: forward dive straight (101A)",
 "category": "parkour-apparatus-balance",
 "time_limit_s": 20.0,
 "stages": [
  "take-off",
  "entry",
  "under water"
 ],
 "rulebook": "World Aquatics (FINA) Diving Rules 2022-2025 (in force 3 December 2022): D 1.5 (dive numbers: 101 = front group, no flying action, one half somersault; A = straight), D 6.5.4 (a double jump on the platform is a failed dive), D 6.5.7 (a twist of 90 degrees or more at the entry: failed), D 6.5.8 / 6.5.8.1 (head first: arms below the head, maximum 4.5 points; the feet entering before the head or hands: failed), D 8.1.1 (awards 0 to 10), D 8.2.4 (standing dives: the starting position on the front end, the feet stay on the platform until the take-off), D 8.4.4 (the take-off bold, balanced and high, from the end of the platform), D 8.5.1-8.5.5 (the direct line of flight; touching the platform; unsafely close: at most 2 points; straight: not bent at the knees or hips, feet together, toes pointed), D 8.6.1-8.6.3 and 8.6.7 (the entry vertical, not twisted, the body straight, feet together, toes pointed; head first with the arms stretched beyond the head in line with the body, the hands close together; the dive is complete when the whole body is under the surface). World Aquatics Facilities Rules FR 5.2 (the 10 m platform at least 6.0 m long and 3.0 m wide, rigid and horizontal, a 0.2 m front edge, projecting at least 1.50 m beyond the pool edge, handrails at the back and the sides up to 1 m from the front edge)",
 "deviations": [
  "D 8.1.1 (the judges' award): the panel's 0-10 award is replaced by pass/fail lines that stand for a dive with no failed-dive call and no capped award: the entry within 15.0 degrees of vertical, hips and knees bent less than 20.0 degrees, the ankles at most 0.12 m apart, the toes pointed (each ankle extended at least 0.3 rad of the robot's 0.52), both palms at least 0.15 m beyond the head along the body line, at most 0.2 m apart and within 0.15 m of the body line, a twist under 20.0 degrees; the rule book gives the qualities, not these numbers",
  "D 8.6 and D 8.1.2 (the entry): the water is not simulated. The pool's surface is a plane the robot passes through without resistance; the entry is judged from the first touch of the surface until the whole body is under it (D 8.6.7), and nothing after that is judged (D 8.1.2: no movement beneath the surface counts). With no water the body keeps turning through the entry (the water stops a diver's rotation), so the entry's angle is taken at one moment, when the hips (the pelvis) reach the surface; the shape is judged through the whole passage",
  "D 8.4.4 (a high take-off): a take-off counts once the robot's centre of mass rises at least 0.1 m above its height at the start, so that a fall forward is not a dive",
  "D 8.5.5 (the straight position): judged from the moment the body line passes the horizontal until the entry (the last quarter somersault; D 8.5.7's quarter somersault for flying dives), with the entry's limits",
  "D 8.5.3 / 8.5.4 / 8.4.4 (touching the platform; unsafely close; a take-off from the end): any touch of the platform or its railings after the take-off fails, and the distance is judged at the water surface: the entry point (the middle of the palms at the first touch of the water) between 0.5 m and 3.0 m in front of the platform's front edge (the rule book leaves 'unsafely close' and 'from the end' to the judges)",
  "D 8.5.1 (the direct line of flight): the entry point must be within 0.5 m to either side of the line the robot faces at the start",
  "D 8.2.4.2 / 6.5.4 (the feet stay on the platform; no double jump): a flight of 0.03 s or more that lands on the platform again fails, and neither foot may be off the platform for more than 0.1 s while the other is on it (a step, a crow-hop)",
  "D 8.2.1 (the starting position): the robot is placed at the front end at t = 0 instead of walking there; it stands as its rest pose stands (the arms in front), not straight with the head erect",
  "D 1.5 (the dive): the half somersault is checked as the body line's turn in the vertical plane from the start to the entry, 180.0 degrees within 45.0",
  "FR 5.3.1 (the pool): the pool's water is a drawn surface over a floor that nothing collides with; the pool's depth and the other boards are not built"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Do a forward dive straight (101A) from the 10 m platform: from your standing start at its front end, take off, turn half a somersault forwards in the straight position and enter the water head first, vertical, with your arms stretched beyond your head.",
  "rules": "You stand on the 10 m platform's front end facing the water (+x). The dive: take off from the platform (both feet stay on it until the take-off: a foot off it for more than 0.1 s while the other is on it, or any flight of 0.03 s or more that lands on the platform again, fails; the take-off counts when you are in the air and your centre of mass has risen at least 0.1 m above its height at the start), turn forwards through half a somersault (180.0 degrees, within 45.0, counted from your upright start) and enter the water head first. From the moment your body line (from the middle of your ankles to the middle of your shoulders) passes the horizontal until the dive is complete you must be in the straight position: hips and knees each bent less than 20.0 degrees from straight, the ankles at most 0.12 m apart, the toes pointed (each ankle pitched at least 0.3 rad towards the toes). The entry starts when the first part of you reaches the water surface (z = 0) and the dive is complete when the whole of you is under it; that first part must be a hand or the head (feet first fails); when your pelvis reaches the surface your body line must be within 15.0 degrees of vertical, head down; through the entry: not twisted (your shoulders' axis within 20.0 degrees of the platform's width direction); both palms at least 0.15 m beyond the centre of your head along the body line, at most 0.2 m apart and each within 0.15 m of the body line; and the straight position. The entry point (the middle of your palms when the first part of you touches the water) must be between 0.5 m and 3.0 m in front of the platform's front edge (closer is unsafely close) and within 0.5 m to either side of the line you face at the start. You fail if, after the take-off, any part of you touches the platform or its railings. Before the take-off any part other than the feet on the platform, or anything on the railings, fails. The water is not simulated: nothing after the dive is complete is judged. Success: a complete dive that keeps all of these. The attempt ends after 20 s.",
  "venue": "The platform's top is 10.0 m above the water, 6.0 m long and 3.0 m wide; its front edge (a 0.2 m thick slab) is 0.119 m ahead of your pelvis, about 2 cm ahead of your toes, square across your path, and you stand on its centre line. It projects 1.5 m beyond the pool's edge; the water lies ahead and below. Handrails 1.0 m high run along both sides of the platform and across its back, the side rails ending 1.0 m before the front edge. The platform is rigid; nothing in the venue moves.",
  "difficulty": "A dive needs a take-off that is high enough and gives exactly the right forward rotation, then a straight body with the legs drawn together, the toes pointed and the short arms stretched overhead through a 1.4 s fall. A small error at the take-off becomes a short or over-rotated entry, and nothing can be corrected once it is in the air."
 },
